#Undercut/Overcut calculator project/idea

#In a race, when two cars are close together, the trailing car can try to jump the lead car 
by pitting earlier (undercut) or later (overcut) than them.

Undercut: you pit first, put on fresh tires. Fresh tires are much faster than worn ones for a 
few laps ("out-lap" + a couple of laps after). If your fresh-tire pace advantage over those laps is 
bigger than the gap you started with, you "jump" the other car when they pit later 
because you were lapping faster while they were still out on old tires.

Overcut: the opposite — you stay out longer on older tires while the rival pits. 
If track position and clean air matter more than tire age at that point, staying out can gain you 
time even though your tires are older.

So in theory, what this will hopefully be able to do is that the calculator answers: "Given the current gap and tire situation, does pitting now gain or lose track position?"

CORE MATH

Pit stop time loss — how many seconds a stop costs at this circuit (entering pit lane, stationary time, rejoining) — varies a lot by track (Monaco ~18-20s, Spa ~20s+, some tracks less)

Tire delta per lap — how much faster a fresh tire is than the current tire, per lap, for the next few laps (this decays as the "rival's" tire also ages, and as your fresh tire's initial advantage fades)

Current gap between the two cars